      "text": "1. A palindrome is a number that reads the same forwards and backwards. For example, the numbers 121, 1331, and 12321 are all palindromes.\n   \n2. We are looking for the smallest positive integer \\( x \\) such that \\( x + 5678 \\) is also a palindrome. This can be expressed symbolically as:\n   \\[\n   x + 5678 = P\n   \\]\n   where \\( P \\) is a palindrome and \\( P > 5678 \\).\n\n3. We will iterate over increasing positive integers \\( x \\) starting from 1, and check if \\( x + 5678 \\) is a palindrome.\n\n4. We will write a function to check if a number is a palindrome. A number is a palindrome if it reads the same forward and backward.\n\nLet's implement this in Python to find the smallest \\( x \\).\n```python\ndef is_palindrome(n):\n    return str(n) == str(n)[::-1]\n\ndef find_smallest_x(base_number):\n    x = 1\n    while True:\n        result = x + base_number\n        if is_palindrome(result):\n            return x\n        x += 1\n\n# We are looking for the smallest positive x such that x + 5678 is a palindrome\nsmallest_x = find_smallest_x(5678)\nprint(smallest_x)\n```\n```output\n731\n```\nThe smallest positive value of \\( x \\) such that \\( x + 5678 \\) results in a palindrome is \\(\\boxed{731}\\).",
